c(1, "header lines 1-7; §2.1 table (verbatim); §2.2 table (verbatim)", "positioning", "Habit Hearts (App Store ID 6737323367; 'Daily Goal & Routine Planner') by Akash Jain (com.habithearts), Productivity / Utilities — an aesthetic habit tracker where tapping fills a heart, with a large icon library, custom habits / colours, presets, reminders, morning / afternoon / evening grouping, quantity and duration targets with partial completion ('if your goal is 120 min, you can tick 20 min of it'), charts, 50+ home-screen widgets, Apple Health, iCloud sync with no sign-in; 'From the makers of Fleur!' (sister apps Fleur budget, Floret planner, Nost); 250 reviews (every one read), 36 storefronts, 2024-11-15 → 2026-09-03, written mean 4.612 (213×5★, 10×4★, 7×3★, 7×2★, 13×1★); external (13 Sep 2026): released 2024-11-11, version 1.2.0 (2026-07-29), free with IAP, 27 languages, iOS 16+, 4+; 1,161 public ratings across the 36 storefronts (written 21.5%); US 4.89 over 287; capabilities (verbatim) " + table("## 2.1 What Habit Hearts is", 0) + " || listing (verbatim) " + table("## 2.2 The listed model", 0) + " — all 21 release notes are the identical string 'Bug fixes and performance improvements'; two different 'Habit Hearts Lifetime' prices ($9.99 and $15.99) plus Monthly $3.99 and Yearly $14.99; reviewers report €10 on sale, €9.99, 899 ₽, $10–16, £10, €25, R$124.75, 129 kr, and one Russian reviewer was quoted 800 ₽ on one card and 1,490 ₽ on another in the same session — 'Price presentation in this app is not stable across users'", "free cap; lifetime / monthly / yearly", "praise", "250 reviews; mean 4.612; US public 4.89 on 287", "none", "header", "app-specific", ["13377613648","13474125877","12245253288"])
c(2, "How to read this table (verbatim); Warnings 1, 2, 6, 7, 8; §1.1 table (verbatim); §1.2; §1.3 table (verbatim); §1.4; §1.5; §1.6; §1.8", "data-caveat", "Method and limits: bands (verbatim) " + table("## How to read this", 0) + " — 897 assignments (3.59 per review), 86 codes in 14 families, 16 unions, read in ten chunks of twenty-five, classification entirely manual (keywords only to test negative claims, folding Latin diacritics and preserving Cyrillic); files (verbatim) " + table("## 1.1 Files used", 0) + "; coverage (verbatim) " + table("## 1.3 Coverage and reconciliation", 0) + " — 40 probed storefronts with zero reviews incl. Japan, China, India, Turkey, Austria, Belgium, Denmark, Saudi Arabia, UAE; is_edited false on every record; one author (Evorita, es) posted twice the same day — at n−1 only ECO_CROSSSELL changes band; one review = 0.4%; only the US (58) reaches 50 (Brazil 31, Russia 29 are leads); no version field and release notes that say nothing — every cause-and-effect claim is inference; 138 reviews (55.20%) non-English across 12 languages, read in original; half the corpus (126, 50.40%) in the first 4.5 months, 2025-01 alone 51; 68 reviews (27.20%) carry almost no product information ('the informative corpus is closer to 180 reviews than 250'); rating is not preference — 13 five-star reviews carry a negative or request code", "n/a", "praise", "250 read; 1 eligible storefront", "research", "method", "generalisable", ["12051634060","12052507920"])
c(3, "Warning 3; §1.7 table (verbatim); §3.5 table (verbatim)", "data-caveat", "An extremely positive corpus: 213 of 250 (85.20%) 5★ and 202 (80.80%) with no complaint and no request; written 4.612 below public (US 4.89 over 287) — the normal direction, small gap; the 37 reviews with any negative code (14.80%) do almost all the diagnostic work; integrity (verbatim) " + table("## 1.7 Review integrity", 0) + " — the Dec–Jan clustering (8 reviews on 27 Dec and 2 Jan; 16 in 1–3 Jan) is seasonal not solicited — it contains six of the 1★ reviews; one review paraphrases the store listing (the only source of iCloud sync praise); 5 reviews used as a support ticket (mean 3.00) — 'the people who could not find a support route'; mixed / structural (verbatim) " + table("## 3.5 Mixed and structural themes", 0) + " — META_LOWINFO rising 23.81% → 36.00%", "n/a", "praise", "85.20% 5★; any_negative 37 (14.80%) 2.57", "none", "high-priority", "generalisable", ["12245253288","12112764195","13321107548","13707083167"])
c(4, "Warning 4; §0.1 table (verbatim); §3.3 N1 table (verbatim); §7.2 tables (verbatim); §8.1 #1; §8.6 #1; part 8 #1; §0.10 #1", "anti-pattern", "The free habit cap is the whole negative story, and the corpus dates the change: PAY_FREE_CAP 24 (9.60%, high-priority, mean 2.12 — the lowest-rated theme of any size), 11 of 13 1★ (84.6%) and 5 of 7 2★ (71.4%); 22 reviewers name the allowance and they fall into three windows (verbatim) " + table("## 0.1 The free habit cap is the whole negative story", 0) + " — every '1' report falls between 23 Dec 2024 and 3 Jan 2025 — thirteen reviewers on six storefronts (br, ca, es, gb, nl, us) — and nobody reports 1 before or after in twenty-two months: 'This app is free but I literally can only make one habit before it corners me into paying'; 'La versión gratuita solo te deja poner 1 hábito, entiendo que no lo dejen ilimitado, pero con solo un hábito me parece inservible'; 'Why download a habit tracker app for the purpose of tracking a single habit?'; version 1.0.8 (shipped 2024-12-18) covers 35 reviews at 3.80 — the only release window below 4.0 — and 1.0.9 (2025-01-03) recovers to 4.74 (verbatim) " + table("## 7.2 The cap change", 1) + " — the Christmas acquisition peak cannot explain why the reported number changed from 3 to 1 and back, nor January 2026 at 4.62 with no '1 habit' reports; full cap timeline (verbatim) " + table("### N1 — The free habit cap", 0) + " — reviewers accept a cap and object to this one ('a free version should not be THAT limited'); two independently: 'At this point it should just be a paid app'; §8.1 #1 'Set and hold the free habit cap at 3 or more. Never 1'; research #1: check remote-config history against 2024-12-18 and 2025-02", "free cap 3 → 1 (23 Dec 2024–Jan 2025) → 3", "1★-burst", "window A 33 @ 4.848 (0% 1–2★); B 75 @ 4.293 (17.3%); C 142 @ 4.725 (4.9%); 1.0.8 35 @ 3.80", "product-rule", "high-priority", "generalisable", ["12097520784","12105105356","12124750581","12130425530","12135234386","12108027572","12111822132","12136488139"])
c(5, "§3.3 N1 window C; §0.10 #2; §8.5 E1", "monetization", "Three is survivable, not comfortable: window C (cap 3) still produces 8 complaints — 'Really pretty and great user interface, but only 3 free habits. Not worth it for me' (3★, 2026); 'your options are practically drink water, meditate, and sleep' (1★, free habits limited to presets — PAY_FREE_PRESET_ONLY); 'Za darmo można zrobić chyba 4 nawyki nie polecam…' (the only cap-of-4 report); two 4★ name the cap calmly ('The only issue I really had was that you can only make 3 habits') — 'the band where the cap costs a star rather than four'; 'um limite de 6 hábitos já estava Ok e reteria muito mais usuários' — the only review stating the category norm (COMP_NORM); §0.10 #2: test 3 against 5–6, not against 1; E1: A/B cap 3 vs 5 vs 6 on trial-start and purchase rate, not review sentiment", "cap 3", "blocked-conversion", "window C PAY_FREE_CAP 8 (5.6%)", "research", "meaningful", "generalisable", ["13706534336","13535961741","14446977408","14328321200","12124750581"])
c(6, "§0.2 table (verbatim); §3.4 P1", "product-rule", "The product is the way it looks: design_praise 152 (60.80%, high-priority, mean 4.75) — three reviews in five (verbatim) " + table("## 0.2 The product is the way it looks", 0) + " — reviewers tie the look to whether they keep using it (OUT_MOTIV 48, 19.20%, stated as a consequence of the design): 'I am someone who needs things to be pleasing to keep doing it, and this gives me that'; 'визуальное оформление располагает к тому, чтобы почаще заходить и тыкать на сердечки'; 'o app faz você realmente querer abrir todo dia'; 'When i usually use a routine app, i give up after a few uses as it feels like a chore. But this app … has a gorgeous layout'; stable across eras (61.11% → 60.81% → 60.00%) and every storefront with ≥4 reviews except Brazil (38.71%); 'the design is not a differentiator to be protected alongside features — it is the feature'; no review mentions streaks — 'the hearts metaphor occupies that slot'", "aesthetic design; hearts metaphor", "5★-burst", "design_praise 152 (60.80%) 4.75; DES_AESTHETIC 99 (39.60%); DES_CUTE 58 (23.20%); DES_ICONS 24", "product-rule", "high-priority", "generalisable", ["12184130776","13249509284","13424755789","12111476547"])
c(7, "§0.3; §3.4 P4", "tactic", "Developer support is the second product, rated 5.00: support_praise 50 (20.00%, high-priority, mean 4.96) — SUP_KIND 45 (18.00%), SUP_FAST 13 (5.20%, 5.00) — 'a rate this analyst has not seen in any other app in this series'; three say a request of theirs shipped (SUP_SHIPPED 3): 'Atienden tus recomendaciones con actualizaciones (en mi caso poder reordenar los hábitos)'; 'a big thank you for adding a feature I requested—I really feel seen and appreciated!'; a review titled 'Notification bug' arrives as a 5★ because 'they were incredibly kind and and patient with me while they worked on a solution'; a 5★ titled 'Support' whose entire body is 'I reached out and they answered almost immediately with a very friendly and kind response'; outcome: bug reports and requests become 5★ reviews; excluding the grant cohort SUP_KIND falls 18.00% → 14.04% and SUP_FAST drops one band — still high", "kind, fast, ships requests", "5★-burst", "support_praise 50 (20.00%) 4.96; SUP_KIND 45; SUP_FAST 13; SUP_SHIPPED 3", "do", "high-priority", "generalisable", ["12628711630","12632945712","14363105130","12251582197"])
c(8, "Warning 5; §0.4 table (verbatim); §1.7 grant table (verbatim); §5.6; §8.1 #4; §8.5 E3; §8.6 #4; part 8 #4; §0.10 #7", "tactic", "Eleven people say they were given premium for free after asking — an asset and a measurement problem: PAY_GRANTED / PAY_GIFTED 11 (4.40%, very strong, all 5★) (verbatim) " + table("## 0.4 Eleven people say they were given premium", 0) + " — six of eleven mention hardship, student status or being between jobs ('I reached out to them about not being able to pay right now due to being in between jobs and they offered a free premium!'; 'developer very generous granted premium access to hs student with no money atm'); 10 of 11 are US; adjacent: one describes the programme, one asks for a discount in the review, two thank the developer for 'access'; grant union 15 (6.00%), every one 5★; outcome: 'this developer converts people who cannot pay into vocal advocates' — and the cohort moves the corpus mean 4.612 → 4.587 (verbatim) " + table("**The grant cohort is the integrity issue", 0) + " — it is invisible: people ask in public, and a 2★ ('I'm to tight for money') and a 1★ price objection are exactly the population it exists for; concentrated in English (Russia: 1 grant, 4 unhappy reviews); §8.1 #4 / E3: surface the hardship route inside the paywall in every shipped language — 'The cheapest available experiment in this whole report'; research #4: does it pay for itself", "free premium on request (hardship / students)", "5★-burst", "PAY_GIFTED 11 (4.40%) 5.00; gift union 15 (6.00%); 10/11 US; mean 4.612 → 4.587 without", "do", "very strong", "generalisable", ["12112630604","12137279733","12164039813","12509417366","13936621361","13450460890","12251848448","12112764195"])
